c. women with HIV/AIDS;
d. women migrant workers;
e. girls;
f. women heads of households;
g. women domestic workers;
h. women home-based workers;
i. elderly women;
j. women informal workers;
k. women in situations of intolerance; and
l. other vulnerable groups of women.

Article 17

(1) Prevention of violence against women is carried out in the following fields:

a. education;
b. health;
c. religion;
d. security;
e. justice;
f. public works and spatial planning;
g. public housing and settlement areas; h. social affairs;
i. manpower;
j. women empowerment and child protection; k. land affairs;
l. environment;
m. population administration and civil registration; n. population control and family planning; o. transportation;
p. communication and informatics;
q. cooperatives, small and medium enterprises;
r. youth and sports;
s. culture;
t. tourism;
u. trade and industry; and
v. other related fields.
(2) Prevention of violence against women as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out in the following forms:
a. developing communication media, information, education and public campaigns through media;
b. developing educational materials and curricula; c. developing safe transportation systems and public spaces;
d. establishing early detection systems, security and integrated complaint services in industrial areas, companies, residential areas, educational environments, Islamic boarding schools and other public spaces;
e. providing consultation services for families, prospective brides and grooms, students, Islamic boarding school students, youth, university students and vulnerable groups;
f. encouraging behavioral change of parents and the community;
g. developing policies related to regencies/cities, villages, schools, Islamic boarding schools, and universities that are safe and friendly for women and integrated with regional spatial planning policies;
h. forming and developing cadres, communities, and groups from among youth, students, Islamic boarding school students, university students, journalists, social media influencers, educators, and religious leaders for prevention;
i. conducting education and advocacy to owners, managers and/or users of social media;
j. developing special protection and support systems for vulnerable groups of women;
k. conducting awareness-raising for perpetrators;
l. conducting cooperation among those responsible for prevention of violence against women; and m. conducting socialization of laws and regulations. (3) The fields and forms of prevention of violence against women as referred to in paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be the responsibility of Regional Apparatus that oversee related affairs.

Article 20

(1) In the implementation of women protection services, the Regional Government establishes P2TP2A as a service network for women victims of violence.

(2) The establishment of P2TP2A is intended so that women victims of violence obtain comprehensive, integrated, quality, inclusive and sustainable services.

(3) P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) has the following duties:

a. providing services to women victims of violence in accordance with standard operating procedures; b. conducting coordination of prevention, services and referrals among government-owned service institutions, community-owned service institutions, professional organizations, law enforcement agencies and other related institutions;
c. conducting data collection on case services;
d. providing various information needed by victims, victims' families and the community;
e. conducting prevention of recurrence of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women; f. conducting monitoring and evaluation of victim services; and
g. providing capacity building, guidance and assistance related to victim services.

