To Enact: REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING EMPOWERMENT AND PROTECTION OF WOMEN.

6.9.text:
Article 17

(1) Prevention of violence against women is carried out in the following fields:

a. education;
b. health;
c. religion;
d. security;
e. justice;
f. public works and spatial planning;
g. public housing and settlement areas; h. social affairs;
i. manpower;
j. women empowerment and child protection; k. land affairs;
l. environment;
m. population administration and civil registration; n. population control and family planning; o. transportation;
p. communication and informatics;
q. cooperatives, small and medium enterprises;
r. youth and sports;
s. culture;
t. tourism;
u. trade and industry; and
v. other related fields.
(2) Prevention of violence against women as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out in the following forms:
a. developing communication media, information, education and public campaigns through media;
b. developing educational materials and curricula; c. developing safe transportation systems and public spaces;
d. establishing early detection systems, security and integrated complaint services in industrial areas, companies, residential areas, educational environments, Islamic boarding schools and other public spaces;
e. providing consultation services for families, prospective brides and grooms, students, Islamic boarding school students, youth, university students and vulnerable groups;
f. encouraging behavioral change of parents and the community;
g. developing policies related to regencies/cities, villages, schools, Islamic boarding schools, and universities that are safe and friendly for women and integrated with regional spatial planning policies;
h. forming and developing cadres, communities, and groups from among youth, students, Islamic boarding school students, university students, journalists, social media influencers, educators, and religious leaders for prevention;
i. conducting education and advocacy to owners, managers and/or users of social media;
j. developing special protection and support systems for vulnerable groups of women;
k. conducting awareness-raising for perpetrators;
l. conducting cooperation among those responsible for prevention of violence against women; and m. conducting socialization of laws and regulations. (3) The fields and forms of prevention of violence against women as referred to in paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be the responsibility of Regional Apparatus that oversee related affairs.

Article 23

(1) Services are implemented to fulfill the rights of women victims of violence and are carried out with the following principles:

a. respect for human rights; b. non-discrimination;
c. confidentiality;
d. empathy;
e. inclusion;
f. empowerment;
g. victim-based decisions;
h. honesty;
i. professionalism;
j. integration; and
k. sustainability.
(2) Services to women victims of violence must consider special conditions and needs based on the vulnerabilities experienced by women as referred to in Article 20

Article 24

(1) Forms of services as referred to in Article 23 include:

a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. social rehabilitation services;
d. legal assistance and law enforcement services; and e. repatriation and social reintegration services. (2) Services for women victims of violence are implemented quickly, free of charge, comprehensively, with quality and integrated with services provided by government agencies, the community, educational institutions, religious institutions, professional institutions, Islamic boarding schools, the business sector through an integrated service system.

Article 29

(1) Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in Article 24 paragraph (1) letter e shall be implemented with the following activities:

a. repatriation and/or pick-up of victims; b. provision of population documents or personal identity;
c. alternative family;
d. scholarships and educational support facilities; e. non-formal education;
f. skills training and economic enterprises; g. financial assistance and business capital;
h. economic enterprise assistance;
i. housing assistance;
j. food and/or nutrition assistance for victims and their families;
k. provision of social protection programs and poverty alleviation programs;
l. other forms of repatriation and social reintegration services needed by victims.
(2) Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be provided to victims, families and persons whose lives depend on victims with the aim that victims have the readiness and ability to live in society.
(3) Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out in an integrated manner with the Integrated Referral Service System for social protection and poverty alleviation and the Child Social Welfare Center.
(4) Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be the responsibility of the Regional Apparatus managing:
a. social affairs;
b. population administration and civil registration affairs;
c. labor affairs;
d. cooperative, small and medium enterprise affairs; e. education affairs;
f. health affairs;
g. public works and spatial planning affairs; h. land affairs, women's empowerment and child protection affairs; and
i. other related affairs.
(5) Further provisions regarding repatriation and social reintegration services shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 3

(1) Every woman has the right to:

a. live and maintain life and improve her standard of living;
b. fulfill her basic needs to grow and develop properly, have a family and procreate;
c. develop her personality, obtain education, and improve her quality of life to become a person who is faithful, devout, responsible, of noble character, happy, and prosperous in accordance with human rights;
d. obtain justice, security, and freedom to express opinions without discrimination;
e. be involved in every stage of the development process; f. be free from slavery or enslavement
g. be free from threats;
h. obtain protection of her person, family, honor, dignity and property rights;
i. obtain welfare and a decent life; j. participate in politics, economics, social and culture;
k. perform legal acts; and
l. freely choose a spouse in her marriage. (2) Every wife during the marriage bond has the same rights and responsibilities as her husband over all matters concerning her married life, relationship with her children, and ownership and management of joint property in accordance with the applicable laws and regulations. (3) After the dissolution of a marriage, a woman has rights and responsibilities with her former husband over all matters concerning the children with due regard to the best interests of the child.

Article 34

Guidance as referred to in Article 32 letter b shall include:

a. providing protection and assistance to develop oneself through improving family capabilities and skills;
b. conducting counseling to improve women's quality of life;
c. conducting counseling to improve gender equality in community family life;
d. increasing access and opportunities to receive information and economic resources through efforts to increase prosperous family income and increase women's economic productivity; and
e. making efforts to empower female heads of households.

Article 16

(1) Prevention of violence against women is intended to:

a. create a safe environment for women; and
b. reduce the vulnerability to violence, exploitation and discrimination for vulnerable groups of women. (2) Vulnerable groups of women as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b are as follows:
a. women in disaster and social conflict situations; b. women with disabilities;
c. women with HIV/AIDS;
d. women migrant workers;
e. girls;
f. women heads of households;
g. women domestic workers;
h. women home-based workers;
i. elderly women;
j. women informal workers;
k. women in situations of intolerance; and
l. other vulnerable groups of women.

Article 27

(1) Social rehabilitation services as referred to in Article 24 paragraph (1) letter c shall be implemented with the following activities:

a. counseling;
b. assistance;
c. linguists and/or translators;
d. spiritual guidance;
e. shelter and/or safe house;
f. temporary shelter;
g. strengthening based on family and/or group support;
h. mobility support for victims with disabilities;
i. provision of special social assistance for victims; and j. other forms of social rehabilitation services needed by victims.
(2) Social rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1) letters a, b, c, d, f, g, h, and i shall be the responsibility of the Regional Apparatus managing women's empowerment and child protection affairs.
(3) Social rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1) letters e and j shall be the responsibility of the Regional Apparatus managing social affairs and women's empowerment and child protection affairs.
(4) Further provisions regarding social rehabilitation services shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

